{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Caw7cLgxjV5Vf9qdkBsNT6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to generate detailed imagined experiment data for all \"planned\" experiments in the experiment plan, based on the \"median\" tier definition they've provided. Each experiment needs expected results with rationale explaining why those results would occur under the median tier assumptions.\n\nLet me go through each planned experiment and generate predictions consistent with the median tier:\n\nKey median tier parameters:\n- delta_over_base_liberoplus_sr_pp: [4, 8]\n- a3_gap_full_vs_no_conformal_pp: [2, 5]\n\n\n- a4_gap_perDOF_vs_scalar_pp: [1, 3]\n- coc_success_spearman_rho: [0.3, 0.5]\n- aci_ellipsoid_volume_narrowing_pct: [10, 20]\n- srpo_stacking_gain_pp: [1, 3]\n- srpo_stacked_absolute_sr: [83, 85]\n- libero_nominal_degradation_pp: [1, 3]\n- crm_per_perturbation_gain_camera_noise_robotstate_pp: [5, 10]\n\nNow I'm looking at the full set of median tier parameter ranges to establish the baseline expectations for each experiment.\n\nI'm laying out my core assumptions: Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage on the K=10 covariance should preserve enough signal for CRM to partially work, per-DOF features mainly help through marginal statistics rather than covariance structure, COC's weak correlation with success limits per-step reliability, CRM gains come from observation conditioning rather than conformal structure specifically, and SRPO already captures most of the correctable error leaving little headroom for stacking improvements.\n\nNow I'm working through the planned experiments, starting with the full ACR-VLA pipeline where I need to establish consistent baseline performance numbers across LIBERO-Plus.\n\nFor the A3 ablation removing conformal features, I'm estimating a 2-5pp gap, so if the full system hits around 71% on LIBERO-Plus, the ablated version would drop to 66-69%.\n\nThe A4 ablation comparing scalar versus per-DOF control shows a 1-3pp difference, putting the scalar variant at 68-70% versus the full 71%.\n\nStacking SRPO on top gives another 1-3pp gain over the 82.1% baseline, reaching 83-85% absolute performance.\n\nFor the COC signal validation, I'm seeing Spearman correlations in the 0.3-0.5 range and ACI ellipsoid volume reductions of 10-20%.\n\nRemoving ACI entirely causes a modest performance drop, while removing CRM but keeping ACI for sample selection loses most of the correction capability.\n\nBreaking down the perturbation effects, camera, noise, and robot-state variations each contribute 5-12pp of gains, whereas layout and language perturbations show minimal impact.\n\nThe joint Mahalanobis approach has a slight edge over Bonferroni correction.\n\nCOC provides a noisier signal than episode-level task success but avoids needing external supervision for ACI.\n\nEpistemic uncertainty features from CGS prove more valuable than aleatoric uncertainty from DiU when dealing with distribution shift.\n\nThe feature importance analysis reveals that CRM leverages conformal features but doesn't apply them in a clean dimension-selective manner.\n\nSnapFlow integration offers a speedup with some quality trade-off.\n\nNow I'm setting up baseline numbers for the YAML output—SmolVLA achieves around 95% success rate on standard LIBERO and drops to roughly 62% on LIBERO-Plus across the harder task levels.\n\nSRPO reaches 82.1% on LIBERO-Plus, which is a substantial improvement over the base. That 20-point gap suggests the base VLA without SRPO sits around 62%, and if ACR-VLA adds 4-8 percentage points on top of that, I'm looking at 66-70% for the median tier performance.\n\nLet me refine these baseline assumptions and work through each experiment's expected performance more carefully. Now I'm looking at the stacking results with SRPO, which gives about a 2 percentage point improvement on top of the base 82%, and I'm also examining the correlation of confidence metrics with a Spearman correlation around 0.4 and volume narrowing around 15%. The per-perturbation breakdown shows camera and noise robustness as the biggest gains at 8-10 points, while language and layout have minimal impact. I'm ready to construct the full YAML results table with the nominal and Plus performance across all tasks. I'm settling on concrete numbers for the LIBERO-Plus results across difficulty levels, aiming for realistic improvements that show ACR-VLA's gains without overstating them. The base performance sits around 62% average, with ACR-VLA pushing that to 68%, while the nominal LIBERO task stays near 93% with a modest 2-point dip when removing conformal prediction.\n\nNow I'm mapping out the specific metrics for each experimental variant—the full pipeline gets the full 6-point boost, the ablation without conformal features drops to 64%, and I'm working through the per-level breakdowns to ensure they're internally consistent. Looking at the coverage metric, ACI is hitting 0.89 on average, just shy of the 0.90 target but with some variance. When I remove ACI entirely and rely only on static calibration, performance drops to 66.5%, about 1.5pp below the full system. Removing the CRM and using just sample selection is even worse at 64%, barely outperforming the baseline. Breaking down the perturbation effects, camera and noise gains are the largest contributors at 8-10pp, while language and layout have minimal impact at 1-2pp. I'm also examining a joint Mahalanobis approach as another variant.\n\nComparing different uncertainty quantification strategies, the joint Mahalanobis method edges out Bonferroni by 1.5pp. For ACI specifically, using episode-level success as the signal slightly outperforms the COC approach by 1.5pp, though COC has the advantage of not requiring external supervision. Finally, looking at aleatoric versus epistemic uncertainty, epistemic uncertainty performs better under distribution shift at 68%, while aleatoric drops to 65%, and combining both gives a marginal 0.5pp improvement.\n\nNow examining feature importance across the 45 dimensions, the top contributors are per-DOF standard deviation for certain joints and conformal radius measures, with ellipsoid volume also playing a role—though the covariance-derived features still matter despite lower rankings. When integrating SnapFlow for faster inference, I'm seeing a 3.2x speedup but at the cost of 1pp success rate drop, with diversity preservation remaining adequate though slightly diminished.\n\nI need to compile the full YAML covering all 13 planned experiments with their detailed results, making sure everything stays consistent across the board.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11185,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11185},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"eac149f7-0379-4dc7-b78d-7f9649494fc8","uuid":"a9fbacd8-50ec-4785-8635-66aca3d64971"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Caw7cLgxjV5Vf9qdkBsNT6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntier: median\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  Claims need recalibration. Claim 1: reframe from \"per-DOF conformal features enable dimension-selective correction\"\n  to \"structured uncertainty features from multivariate CP provide complementary conditioning signal for learned\n  action correction.\" De-emphasize per-DOF vs scalar ablation (A4), emphasize conformal-vs-none ablation (A3).\n  Claim 2: downgrade COC from \"reliable self-referential signal\" to \"lightweight proxy enabling online adaptation\n  without external supervision\" — emphasize the no-supervision property rather than per-step reliability.\n  Claim 3: reframe as \"complementary in principle\" with caveat that training-time methods capture most low-hanging fruit.\n  Shift headline to CGS+CRM framework contribution. Feature importance analysis shows CRM uses conformal features\n  but not in a neatly dimension-selective way. Still a method paper, positioned more as architectural contribution\n  than empirical leap.\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: \"ACR-VLA Full Pipeline on SmolVLA\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_001\"\n    description: >\n      Full ACR-VLA pipeline: CGS (K=10, Ledoit-Wolf) + ACI/COC + CRM (131K params).\n      CRM trained on LIBERO demos + synthetic augmentation (camera jitter, Gaussian action noise, robot-state perturbation).\n      Evaluated on LIBERO 10-task nominal suite and LIBERO-Plus L3–L5.\n    expected_results:\n      base_smolvla_libero_nominal_sr_pct: 95.0\n      acrvla_libero_nominal_sr_pct: 93.0\n      libero_nominal_degradation_pp: 2.0\n      base_smolvla_liberoplus_avg_sr_pct: 62.0\n      base_smolvla_liberoplus_L3_sr_pct: 72.0\n      base_smolvla_liberoplus_L4_sr_pct: 60.0\n      base_smolvla_liberoplus_L5_sr_pct: 54.0\n      acrvla_liberoplus_avg_sr_pct: 68.0\n      acrvla_liberoplus_L3_sr_pct: 78.0\n      acrvla_liberoplus_L4_sr_pct: 66.0\n      acrvla_liberoplus_L5_sr_pct: 60.0\n      delta_over_base_liberoplus_sr_pp: 6.0\n      crm_inference_latency_ms: 1.8\n      cgs_inference_latency_ms: 12.5\n      total_pipeline_overhead_pct: 8.0\n    result_rationale: >\n      Median tier assumes CRM learns a useful correction but most gain comes from the observation-conditioned residual\n      pathway, not from conformal feature conditioning specifically. The 6pp gain over base reflects: (a) CRM successfully\n      learns to correct high-error actions using observation context (~3.5pp), (b) conformal features provide additional\n      complementary signal (~2.5pp via A3 gap), (c) ACI online adaptation contributes modest further improvement (~0.5-1pp).\n      K=10 covariance is noisy — Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage rescues the Mahalanobis ellipsoid from collapse but the\n      conformal radii carry less precise directional information than oracle covariance would. The 2pp nominal degradation\n      comes from CRM occasionally over-correcting on in-distribution steps where conformal features show mild uncertainty\n      (false positive corrections). Synthetic augmentation covers camera/noise/robot-state well but cannot cover layout\n      or language perturbations, limiting L5 gains. The pipeline clears the P0 threshold (>4pp gain) but falls short of\n      the optimistic tier's 8-12pp that would require conformal features to provide genuinely dimension-selective correction.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 1: Main Results — LIBERO and LIBERO-Plus Success Rate\"\n        content: |\n          | Method               | LIBERO (nom.) | LP-L3  | LP-L4  | LP-L5  | LP-Avg |\n          |----------------------|---------------|--------|--------|--------|--------|\n          | SmolVLA (base)       | 95.0          | 72.0   | 60.0   | 54.0   | 62.0   |\n          | + ACR-VLA (ours)     | 93.0          | 78.0   | 66.0   | 60.0   | 68.0   |\n          | Δ                    | -2.0          | +6.0   | +6.0   | +6.0   | +6.0   |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 2a: Bar chart comparing SmolVLA base vs ACR-VLA across LIBERO nominal and LIBERO-Plus L3/L4/L5. Shows consistent +6pp on all LP levels with -2pp nominal trade-off.\"\n      - \"Fig 2b: Per-task breakdown heatmap on LIBERO nominal (10 tasks × 2 methods). Shows ACR-VLA matches or slightly underperforms base on most tasks, with 1-3pp drops on 3-4 tasks where CRM over-corrects.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The 6pp average gain is real but moderate. Uniform improvement across L3-L5 suggests CRM learns a general\n      correction rather than dimension-specific adaptation — consistent with median tier's hypothesis that the gain\n      is primarily observation-conditioned. The 2pp nominal degradation is within acceptable range but noteworthy;\n      a gating mechanism or confidence threshold on CRM activation could reduce it. The result supports reframing\n      Claim 1 around \"structured uncertainty as complementary conditioning signal\" rather than \"dimension-selective correction.\"\n\n  - name: \"Ablation A3 — CRM without Conformal Features\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_002\"\n    description: >\n      CRM receives only observation features (visual embedding + proprioception + base action) but no CGS-derived\n      conformal statistics. Equivalent to an A2C2-style generic learned residual. MAKE-OR-BREAK for Claim 1.\n    expected_results:\n      a3_no_conformal_liberoplus_avg_sr_pct: 64.5\n      a3_gap_full_vs_no_conformal_pp: 3.5\n      a3_no_conformal_libero_nominal_sr_pct: 94.0\n      a3_no_conformal_liberoplus_L3_sr_pct: 74.0\n      a3_no_conformal_liberoplus_L4_sr_pct: 63.0\n      a3_no_conformal_liberoplus_L5_sr_pct: 56.5\n    result_rationale: >\n      The 3.5pp gap (64.5% vs 68.0%) confirms conformal features contribute meaningful signal beyond pure\n      observation-conditioned residual learning, but the gap is modest — in the [2,5] range rather than the >5pp\n      that would indicate strong dimension-selective correction. The A3 variant still achieves +2.5pp over bare\n      base (62%), showing that CRM's observation-conditioned residual learning captures substantial value independently.\n      This is the median tier's core insight: CRM is architecturally valuable as a learned correction module, and\n      conformal features improve it, but conformal conditioning is a complementary signal rather than the primary\n      driver. The gap passes the kill criterion (>2pp) so Claim 1 survives but requires reframing. Nominal\n      degradation is slightly lower (1pp vs 2pp) because without conformal features, CRM is less aggressive in\n      its corrections, producing fewer false-positive over-corrections on in-distribution data.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 2 (row): A3 ablation within main ablation table\"\n        content: |\n          | Variant                    | LIBERO (nom.) | LP-Avg | Δ vs Full |\n          |----------------------------|---------------|--------|-----------|\n          | ACR-VLA (full)             | 93.0          | 68.0   | —         |\n          | A3: CRM w/o conformal      | 94.0          | 64.5   | -3.5      |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 3 (ablation bar chart): A3 bar adjacent to full ACR-VLA and base. Clearly shows A3 intermediate between base and full, with 3.5pp gap. Error bars (95% CI over 3 seeds) likely overlap partially — gap is statistically significant but not dramatic.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The 3.5pp gap is the most important number in the paper. It clears the 2pp kill criterion but sits squarely\n      in the median range. In the paper, this should be presented as: \"removing conformal features reduces\n      LIBERO-Plus SR by 3.5pp, confirming that CGS-derived uncertainty statistics provide complementary conditioning\n      signal for CRM beyond what observation features alone can supply.\" Avoid framing as \"enabling dimension-selective\n      correction\" since the gap size and feature importance analysis (plan_012) won't support that stronger claim.\n      Reviewers may note that 3.5pp with overlapping CIs is modest — prepare to emphasize that this is a consistent\n      improvement across perturbation types and seeds, plus the architectural contribution argument.\n\n  - name: \"Ablation A4 — CRM with Scalar Uncertainty\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_003\"\n    description: >\n      CRM receives scalar uncertainty (Euclidean distance from calibration set mean, à la ReconVLA) instead of\n      45D per-DOF conformal features. Tests whether per-DOF structure provides value beyond a single scalar.\n    expected_results:\n      a4_scalar_liberoplus_avg_sr_pct: 66.0\n      a4_gap_perDOF_vs_scalar_pp: 2.0\n      a4_scalar_libero_nominal_sr_pct: 93.5\n      a4_scalar_liberoplus_L3_sr_pct: 76.0\n      a4_scalar_liberoplus_L4_sr_pct: 64.0\n      a4_scalar_liberoplus_L5_sr_pct: 58.0\n    result_rationale: >\n      The 2pp gap (66% vs 68%) confirms per-DOF features are better than scalar, but the margin is slim — consistent\n      with median tier's assumption that per-DOF advantage comes primarily through marginal information (per-DOF std\n      and radius independently) rather than cross-DOF covariance structure. A scalar summary statistic already\n      captures \"how uncertain is this action overall,\" which accounts for much of the useful signal. The remaining\n      2pp comes from CRM having access to which specific DOFs are uncertain, enabling modestly better residual\n      allocation. But because K=10 covariance is noisy, the rich covariance structure that would enable truly\n      dimension-selective correction is attenuated. The scalar variant (66%) vs no-conformal variant (64.5%) shows\n      only 1.5pp gap between scalar and no uncertainty — meaning even a crude uncertainty scalar helps, and the\n      per-DOF structure adds incrementally on top. This stacking of gaps (A3 → A4 → full) is consistent but\n      none of the individual gaps are large.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 2 (row): A4 ablation\"\n        content: |\n          | Variant                     | LIBERO (nom.) | LP-Avg | Δ vs Full |\n          |---------------------------- |---------------|--------|-----------|\n          | ACR-VLA (full, 45D)         | 93.0          | 68.0   | —         |\n          | A4: CRM w/ scalar uncert.   | 93.5          | 66.0   | -2.0      |\n          | A3: CRM w/o conformal       | 94.0          | 64.5   | -3.5      |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 3 (extended): Stacked bar or grouped bar showing base → A3 → A4 → full progression. Visualizes the incremental contribution: observation residual (+2.5), scalar uncertainty (+1.5), per-DOF structure (+2.0).\"\n    analysis: >\n      The A4 gap (2pp) is modest and should be de-emphasized in the paper per the narrative guidance. Rather than\n      claiming \"per-DOF > scalar,\" present it as part of the ablation ladder: \"uncertainty features improve CRM\n      regardless of representation (scalar +1.5pp, structured 45D +3.5pp over no uncertainty), with structured\n      features providing additional value through per-DOF granularity.\" The 2pp gap may not survive reviewer scrutiny\n      as a standalone claim — it likely requires 5+ seeds to achieve statistical significance. In the median tier,\n      this becomes supporting evidence rather than a headline result.\n\n  - name: \"ACR-VLA Stacked on SRPO\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_004\"\n    description: >\n      Train a new CRM on SRPO-finetuned SmolVLA checkpoint using LIBERO demos. Evaluate on LIBERO-Plus to test\n      orthogonal complementarity with training-time optimization (Claim 3).\n    expected_results:\n      srpo_base_liberoplus_avg_sr_pct: 82.1\n      srpo_acrvla_liberoplus_avg_sr_pct: 84.0\n      srpo_stacking_gain_pp: 1.9\n      srpo_acrvla_libero_nominal_sr_pct: 96.5\n      srpo_base_libero_nominal_sr_pct: 97.0\n      srpo_nominal_degradation_pp: 0.5\n      srpo_acrvla_liberoplus_L3_sr_pct: 89.0\n      srpo_acrvla_liberoplus_L4_sr_pct: 83.0\n      srpo_acrvla_liberoplus_L5_sr_pct: 80.0\n    result_rationale: >\n      SRPO's training-time optimization has already captured most of the correctable error — its 82.1% baseline\n      is 20pp above the base SmolVLA's 62%. The residual errors at 82.1% are predominantly from perturbation types\n      that synthetic augmentation cannot cover (layout changes, language variation) and from hard task instances\n      where the observation itself is insufficient. CRM's observation-conditioned residual finds less room to\n      improve because SRPO has already learned robust representations. The conformal features are also less\n      informative because SRPO's action distribution is already tighter under perturbation (lower epistemic\n      uncertainty → less signal for CGS). The 1.9pp stacking gain is real but marginal — consistent with median\n      tier's \"complementary in principle\" framing. The gain comes primarily from camera and noise perturbations\n      where synthetic augmentation provides coverage and CRM can still make useful corrections on top of SRPO.\n      Nominal degradation is only 0.5pp because SRPO's stronger base representations make CRM's false-positive\n      over-corrections less likely. The absolute 84.0% sets a new SOTA but the marginal gain is not compelling\n      as a standalone selling point.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 3: Stacking on SRPO\"\n        content: |\n          | Method                  | LIBERO (nom.) | LP-Avg | LP-L3 | LP-L4 | LP-L5 |\n          |-------------------------|---------------|--------|-------|-------|-------|\n          | SRPO (SOTA)             | 97.0          | 82.1   | 87.0  | 81.0  | 78.3  |\n          | SRPO + ACR-VLA (ours)   | 96.5          | 84.0   | 89.0  | 83.0  | 80.0  |\n          | Δ                       | -0.5          | +1.9   | +2.0  | +2.0  | +1.7  |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 4: Comparison chart showing ACR-VLA on SmolVLA base (+6pp) vs ACR-VLA on SRPO (+1.9pp). Illustrates diminishing returns as base improves, supporting 'complementary in principle' framing.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The 1.9pp stacking gain falls below the 2pp threshold in the kill criterion for Claim 3, putting the claim\n      in a borderline position. With standard error bars over 3 seeds, 1.9pp may or may not be statistically\n      significant. In the median tier, Claim 3 is downgraded to \"complementary in principle\" — the paper should\n      acknowledge that SRPO captures most correctable error and position stacking as demonstrating architectural\n      compatibility rather than a compelling empirical lift. The headline result shifts to the +6pp on base SmolVLA\n      as the primary contribution, with SRPO stacking as a secondary data point. The new SOTA of 84.0% is still\n      reportable but should not be the paper's primary selling point.\n\n  - name: \"COC Signal Validation\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_005\"\n    description: >\n      Validate Chunk Overlap Consistency as self-referential ACI signal: (a) Spearman ρ(COC coverage fraction,\n      task success) across episodes, (b) ACI α_t convergence trajectory, (c) ACI-adapted ellipsoid volume vs\n      static calibration at matched empirical coverage.\n    expected_results:\n      coc_success_spearman_rho: 0.40\n      coc_success_spearman_p_value: 0.002\n      aci_converges_within_episodes: 120\n      aci_steady_state_alpha: 0.12\n      aci_target_coverage: 0.90\n      aci_achieved_coverage_mean: 0.88\n      aci_achieved_coverage_std: 0.04\n      ellipsoid_volume_static_calibration: 1.0\n      ellipsoid_volume_aci_adapted_relative: 0.85\n      aci_ellipsoid_volume_narrowing_pct: 15.0\n      coc_per_step_accuracy_as_binary_classifier: 0.62\n    result_rationale: >\n      COC correlates with task success at ρ=0.40 — statistically significant but weak. Episodes with consistently\n      high COC agreement (overlapping action chunks agree with each other) tend to succeed more often, but the\n      per-step signal is noisy (62% accuracy as binary success predictor). This is because chunk overlap measures\n      temporal consistency of the policy, which correlates with but does not cause success — a policy can be\n      consistently wrong (high COC, low success) or succeed despite occasional inconsistency. ACI converges\n      within ~120 episodes because the weak but consistent signal is sufficient for slow exponential-moving-average\n      adaptation of the conformal threshold α_t. The 15% ellipsoid volume narrowing shows ACI tightens prediction\n      sets modestly compared to static calibration — it detects distribution shift and adjusts, but the noisy\n      COC signal prevents aggressive tightening. Achieved coverage (88%) undershoots the 90% target slightly\n      due to COC noise causing occasional over-tightening. This profile supports reframing COC as \"lightweight\n      proxy enabling online adaptation without external supervision\" — the key value is not per-step reliability\n      but the ability to adapt without any ground-truth task success signal.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 4: COC Signal Quality\"\n        content: |\n          | Metric                              | Value         |\n          |-------------------------------------|---------------|\n          | Spearman ρ (COC, task success)       | 0.40 (p=.002) |\n          | COC per-step binary accuracy         | 62%           |\n          | ACI convergence episodes             | ~120          |\n          | ACI target / achieved coverage       | 90% / 88±4%  |\n          | Ellipsoid volume narrowing (vs static)| 15%          |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 5a: Scatter plot of episode-level COC coverage fraction vs binary task success. Shows positive trend (ρ=0.40) with substantial scatter — many episodes with high COC still fail, and some low-COC episodes succeed.\"\n      - \"Fig 5b: ACI α_t convergence trajectory over 200 episodes. Shows initial oscillation (ep 0-50), gradual convergence (ep 50-120), and steady-state fluctuation around α=0.12 (ep 120+).\"\n      - \"Fig 5c: Violin plot comparing ellipsoid volumes under static calibration vs ACI-adapted, at matched ~88% empirical coverage. ACI distribution is tighter (15% narrower) but with longer tail.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The ρ=0.40 result is the best the paper can honestly report for COC as a self-referential signal. It clears\n      the 0.3 kill threshold but is far from the 0.6+ that would support \"reliable\" characterization. The paper\n      should position this as: \"COC provides a lightweight, supervision-free proxy for ACI adaptation that achieves\n      meaningful ellipsoid tightening (15%) without requiring any external task success signal — trading per-step\n      reliability for complete autonomy.\" The 62% per-step accuracy should be reported honestly as a limitation.\n      The convergence within 120 episodes is adequate for deployment scenarios but means ACI is a slow-adaptation\n      mechanism, not a real-time reliability signal.\n\n  - name: \"Ablation A1 — CRM without ACI (Static Calibration)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_006\"\n    description: >\n      CRM with static CGS calibration (fixed α from calibration set, no ACI online adaptation).\n      Isolates the contribution of ACI/COC online threshold adjustment.\n    expected_results:\n      a1_static_liberoplus_avg_sr_pct: 66.5\n      a1_static_libero_nominal_sr_pct: 93.0\n      a1_gap_vs_full_pp: 1.5\n      a1_static_liberoplus_L3_sr_pct: 77.0\n      a1_static_liberoplus_L4_sr_pct: 65.0\n      a1_static_liberoplus_L5_sr_pct: 57.5\n    result_rationale: >\n      ACI's online adaptation contributes a modest 1.5pp over static calibration. Static CGS with a fixed threshold\n      calibrated on the nominal distribution still produces useful conformal features — the per-DOF std and radius\n      estimates are driven primarily by sample diversity rather than threshold tuning. Under distribution shift,\n      static calibration's conformal sets are overly conservative (too wide ellipsoids), meaning CRM receives\n      inflated uncertainty features that reduce its correction precision. ACI's 15% volume narrowing translates\n      to CRM making slightly more targeted corrections. However, the improvement is modest because (a) CRM can\n      partially compensate for miscalibrated features by learning to discount inflated values during training,\n      and (b) the ACI adaptation is itself noisy (COC ρ=0.40), so it only partially corrects the calibration\n      error. The 1.5pp gap is consistent across perturbation levels, suggesting ACI provides uniform rather than\n      perturbation-specific improvement.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 2 (row): A1 ablation\"\n        content: |\n          | Variant                    | LIBERO (nom.) | LP-Avg | Δ vs Full |\n          |----------------------------|---------------|--------|-----------|\n          | ACR-VLA (full)             | 93.0          | 68.0   | —         |\n          | A1: Static calibration     | 93.0          | 66.5   | -1.5      |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 3 (ablation chart, A1 bar): Shows A1 slightly below full system. The gap is small but consistent across seeds.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The 1.5pp gap is small enough that ACI is a \"nice to have\" rather than a critical component. This is consistent\n      with median tier: COC is noisy, so ACI's adaptation is limited. In the paper, present ACI as providing\n      \"principled online recalibration\" with the honest caveat that the empirical gain is modest. The value\n      proposition is more about the theoretical coverage guarantee than the empirical SR improvement.\n\n  - name: \"Ablation A2 — ACI without CRM (Sample Selection Only)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_007\"\n    description: >\n      CGS + COC/ACI used for sample selection (pick lowest-uncertainty sample from K=10 candidates) without\n      CRM residual correction. Extends ReconVLA's approach to multivariate conformal setting.\n    expected_results:\n      a2_selection_liberoplus_avg_sr_pct: 64.0\n      a2_selection_libero_nominal_sr_pct: 95.5\n      a2_gap_vs_full_pp: 4.0\n      a2_gap_vs_base_pp: 2.0\n      a2_selection_liberoplus_L3_sr_pct: 73.5\n      a2_selection_liberoplus_L4_sr_pct: 62.0\n      a2_selection_liberoplus_L5_sr_pct: 56.5\n    result_rationale: >\n      Sample selection from K=10 flow-matching candidates provides modest 2pp improvement over base — CGS\n      identifies which samples have lower joint nonconformity and selecting them avoids the worst outlier\n      actions. But this approach is fundamentally limited: it can only select among existing samples, not\n      synthesize a correction. When all 10 samples are biased in the same direction (common under systematic\n      perturbation), selection cannot help. CRM's learned residual adds the crucial ability to push the action\n      toward a corrected target, accounting for the remaining 4pp gap to full ACR-VLA. The near-zero nominal\n      degradation (-0.5pp from 95.5% vs 95.0%) is because sample selection is conservative — it rarely makes\n      things worse since it's choosing among valid samples. This ablation is the strongest evidence for CRM's\n      value: the learned correction module provides 4pp of the total 6pp gain, while sample selection provides only 2pp.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 2 (row): A2 ablation\"\n        content: |\n          | Variant                        | LIBERO (nom.) | LP-Avg | Δ vs Full | Δ vs Base |\n          |-------------------------------|---------------|--------|-----------|-----------|\n          | ACR-VLA (full)                | 93.0          | 68.0   | —         | +6.0      |\n          | A2: Sample selection only      | 95.5          | 64.0   | -4.0      | +2.0      |\n          | SmolVLA (base)                | 95.0          | 62.0   | -6.0      | —         |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 3 (ablation chart, A2 bar): A2 is close to base, far from full system. Visually makes the case for CRM's importance.\"\n    analysis: >\n      This ablation is critical for the paper's architecture narrative. It shows that the CRM learned correction\n      module — not sample selection — is the primary source of improvement. Combined with A3 (CRM w/o conformal\n      features at 64.5%), it reveals an interesting decomposition: sample selection (+2pp) ≈ CRM w/o conformal (+2.5pp),\n      meaning CGS-informed sample selection and observation-conditioned residual provide similar value. CRM with\n      conformal features (+6pp total) outperforms both, confirming the value of combining learned correction with\n      structured uncertainty conditioning.\n\n  - name: \"Per-Perturbation Breakdown\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_008\"\n    description: >\n      ACR-VLA gain broken down across 7 LIBERO-Plus perturbation dimensions: camera jitter, observation noise,\n      robot-state perturbation, lighting, background, layout, language. Tests hypothesis that gains concentrate\n      on augmentation-covered perturbations.\n    expected_results:\n      gain_camera_jitter_pp: 9.0\n      gain_observation_noise_pp: 10.0\n      gain_robot_state_pp: 7.0\n      gain_lighting_pp: 5.0\n      gain_background_pp: 4.0\n      gain_layout_pp: 2.0\n      gain_language_pp: 1.0\n      avg_augmented_perturbations_gain_pp: 8.7\n      avg_non_augmented_perturbations_gain_pp: 2.3\n    result_rationale: >\n      Gains strongly correlate with synthetic augmentation coverage. Camera jitter (+9pp) and observation noise\n      (+10pp) are directly covered by training-time augmentation, so CRM has seen similar perturbations during\n      training and learns effective corrections. Robot-state perturbation (+7pp) is partially covered (joint-level\n      noise augmentation captures some but not all dynamics changes). Lighting (+5pp) and background (+4pp) are\n      visual perturbations where CRM benefits from visual encoder features detecting anomalous observations,\n      even without explicit augmentation. Layout (+2pp) and language (+1pp) show minimal improvement because\n      these perturbation types change task semantics in ways that neither synthetic augmentation nor conformal\n      statistics can address — a rearranged scene or rephrased instruction requires different behavior, not\n      corrected behavior. The camera/noise/robot-state average of 8.7pp is strong and within the [5,12] range\n      specified for median tier. The layout/language near-zero gain is an honest limitation that should be\n      reported transparently.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 5: Per-Perturbation ACR-VLA Gain\"\n        content: |\n          | Perturbation     | Base SR | ACR-VLA SR | Δ (pp)  | Augmented? |\n          |------------------|---------|------------|---------|------------|\n          | Camera jitter    | 58.0    | 67.0       | +9.0    | ✓          |\n          | Obs. noise       | 55.0    | 65.0       | +10.0   | ✓          |\n          | Robot-state      | 60.0    | 67.0       | +7.0    | ✓ (partial)|\n          | Lighting         | 65.0    | 70.0       | +5.0    | ✗          |\n          | Background       | 68.0    | 72.0       | +4.0    | ✗          |\n          | Layout           | 62.0    | 64.0       | +2.0    | ✗          |\n          | Language         | 66.0    | 67.0       | +1.0    | ✗          |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 6: Grouped bar chart (7 perturbation types × base/ACR-VLA). Color-coded by augmentation coverage (green=covered, yellow=partial, red=uncovered). Clear visual pattern: gains decrease from left to right as augmentation coverage decreases.\"\n      - \"Fig 6 inset: Scatter plot of augmentation coverage (binary/partial) vs SR gain. Shows strong correlation, supporting the honest-limitations narrative.\"\n    analysis: >\n      This breakdown is both a strength and vulnerability. Strength: camera/noise/robot-state gains are substantial\n      (7-10pp) and clearly demonstrate ACR-VLA's value for the perturbation types it's designed to handle. Vulnerability:\n      layout/language near-zero gains reveal that ACR-VLA (and CGS more broadly) cannot address semantic perturbations.\n      Reviewers will likely probe this limitation. The paper should position it honestly: \"ACR-VLA is most effective\n      for perturbations that induce distributional shift in the action space without changing task semantics — camera\n      distortion, sensor noise, and kinematic perturbation. For perturbations that alter scene layout or language\n      instructions, training-time methods (SRPO, RobustVLA) that modify the policy's learned representations are\n      more appropriate.\" The per-perturbation SRPO stacking breakdown should also be reported (plan_004 subset)\n      to show whether stacking helps specifically on the augmentation-covered perturbations.\n\n  - name: \"Ablation A5 — Joint Mahalanobis vs Bonferroni Correction\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_009\"\n    description: >\n      Compare CGS with joint Mahalanobis nonconformity score vs per-DOF Bonferroni correction\n      (independent conformal intervals per dimension with α/7 correction). Tests whether the\n      multivariate CP formulation adds value over independent per-DOF intervals.\n    expected_results:\n      a5_bonferroni_liberoplus_avg_sr_pct: 66.5\n      a5_joint_mahalanobis_liberoplus_avg_sr_pct: 68.0\n      a5_gap_joint_vs_bonferroni_pp: 1.5\n      bonferroni_ellipsoid_volume_ratio_vs_joint: 1.35\n      bonferroni_coverage_achieved_pct: 93.0\n      joint_coverage_achieved_pct: 90.0\n    result_rationale: >\n      Joint Mahalanobis provides 1.5pp advantage over Bonferroni. In the median tier, K=10 covariance is noisy,\n      so the Mahalanobis ellipsoid is not dramatically tighter than the Bonferroni hypercube. With oracle\n      covariance, the joint approach would exploit cross-DOF correlations to produce much tighter ellipsoids;\n      with K=10 and Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage, the covariance estimate is regularized toward the identity, partially\n      undoing the advantage. Bonferroni's α/7 correction makes each marginal interval conservative, leading to\n      35% larger total prediction set volume and 93% coverage (above 90% target — over-coverage wastes\n      information). The joint approach's tighter ellipsoid gives CRM more precise conformal features, particularly\n      for DOFs with strong correlations (e.g., shoulder-elbow joint coupling). But the advantage is modest because\n      Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage attenuates exactly the cross-DOF correlation structure that makes joint Mahalanobis\n      superior to Bonferroni.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 2 (row): A5 ablation\"\n        content: |\n          | Variant                         | LP-Avg | Coverage | Volume Ratio |\n          |---------------------------------|--------|----------|-------------|\n          | CGS (joint Mahalanobis)         | 68.0   | 90%      | 1.00        |\n          | A5: Bonferroni per-DOF          | 66.5   | 93%      | 1.35        |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig S1 (supplementary): Ellipsoid vs Bonferroni hypercube visualization for representative 2-DOF slice (DOF 1 vs DOF 4). Shows tighter joint ellipsoid capturing correlation structure vs axis-aligned rectangle.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The 1.5pp gap is modest and supports the median tier narrative. The advantage exists but is attenuated by\n      K=10 covariance estimation noise. This is a P2 ablation that belongs in supplementary material. It validates\n      the design choice for joint Mahalanobis but does not constitute a major finding. If reviewers push on K=10\n      covariance reliability, this ablation shows the approach still beats the simpler alternative despite\n      estimation noise, which is a reasonable defense.\n\n  - name: \"Ablation A6 — COC vs Episode-Level Success for ACI\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_010\"\n    description: >\n      Compare ACI updated with COC (self-referential, no supervision) vs ACI updated with\n      episode-level binary task success (oracle signal). Tests whether COC's noise penalty\n      is offset by its supervision-free property.\n    expected_results:\n      a6_coc_aci_liberoplus_avg_sr_pct: 68.0\n      a6_oracle_aci_liberoplus_avg_sr_pct: 69.5\n      a6_gap_oracle_vs_coc_pp: 1.5\n      a6_coc_aci_convergence_episodes: 120\n      a6_oracle_aci_convergence_episodes: 40\n      a6_coc_coverage_std: 0.04\n      a6_oracle_coverage_std: 0.02\n    result_rationale: >\n      Oracle episode-level success provides a cleaner ACI signal, achieving 1.5pp higher SR and converging 3×\n      faster (40 vs 120 episodes). This is expected: binary success is a direct, noise-free indicator of whether\n      the policy is performing well, while COC is a proxy that correlates weakly (ρ=0.40). The coverage standard\n      deviation is also halved with oracle signal (0.02 vs 0.04), meaning ACI is more stable. However, the\n      performance gap is only 1.5pp, which demonstrates that COC's weak but consistent correlation with success\n      is sufficient for ACI to eventually converge to a reasonable threshold. The key argument for COC is not\n      performance parity but the supervision-free property: episode-level success requires an external reward\n      signal that is unavailable during deployment. COC operates on the policy's own output consistency, making\n      it applicable without any environment reward. The 1.5pp price of supervision-freedom is a reasonable\n      trade-off for deployment autonomy.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 4 (extended): ACI Signal Comparison\"\n        content: |\n          | ACI Signal         | LP-Avg | Convergence | Coverage σ | Supervision |\n          |--------------------|--------|-------------|------------|-------------|\n          | COC (ours)         | 68.0   | ~120 ep     | 0.04       | None        |\n          | Episode success    | 69.5   | ~40 ep      | 0.02       | Required    |\n          | Δ                  | -1.5   | —           | —          | —           |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig S2a: ACI α_t convergence curves for COC vs oracle signal. Oracle converges faster and with less oscillation.\"\n      - \"Fig S2b: Running coverage (50-episode window) for both signals. Oracle maintains tighter band around 90% target.\"\n    analysis: >\n      This ablation validates the design choice of COC for ACI. The 1.5pp gap is the \"price of autonomy\" — the\n      paper should frame it as: \"COC trades 1.5pp in SR for complete independence from external supervision,\n      enabling ACI adaptation during deployment without environment reward access.\" This is a stronger argument\n      than claiming COC is a \"reliable\" signal — it's a sufficient signal for a task (slow threshold adaptation)\n      that is tolerant of noise.\n\n  - name: \"Ablation A7 — DiU Aleatoric vs CGS Epistemic Features\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_011\"\n    description: >\n      Compare CRM conditioned on: (a) CGS epistemic features (45D, full system), (b) DiU single-pass aleatoric\n      features (7D variance estimate), (c) combined epistemic+aleatoric (52D). Tests whether epistemic uncertainty\n      from conformal prediction provides distinct value over aleatoric uncertainty estimation.\n    expected_results:\n      a7_cgs_epistemic_45d_liberoplus_avg_sr_pct: 68.0\n      a7_diu_aleatoric_7d_liberoplus_avg_sr_pct: 65.0\n      a7_combined_52d_liberoplus_avg_sr_pct: 68.5\n      a7_gap_epistemic_vs_aleatoric_pp: 3.0\n      a7_combined_vs_epistemic_pp: 0.5\n      a7_diu_libero_nominal_sr_pct: 94.5\n      a7_combined_libero_nominal_sr_pct: 92.5\n    result_rationale: >\n      CGS epistemic features outperform DiU aleatoric features by 3pp on LIBERO-Plus. Under distribution shift,\n      epistemic uncertainty rises (the model encounters unfamiliar observations, producing diverse action samples\n      with high inter-sample variance), providing a strong signal for CRM to detect and correct. DiU's aleatoric\n      estimate captures intrinsic action variance that is present even in-distribution — this is useful for\n      identifying inherently ambiguous actions but does not specifically detect distribution shift. On LIBERO-Plus,\n      where the challenge is distribution shift rather than intrinsic ambiguity, epistemic features are more\n      informative. The combined 52D model shows only 0.5pp improvement over epistemic-only, suggesting the\n      aleatoric signal is largely redundant when epistemic features are available — epistemic uncertainty subsumes\n      the actionable information in aleatoric estimates under shift. The combined model also shows slightly more\n      nominal degradation (92.5% vs 93.0%) due to the additional aleatoric features sometimes triggering\n      unnecessary corrections on in-distribution data where aleatoric uncertainty is naturally elevated.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 2 (row): A7 ablation\"\n        content: |\n          | Variant                    | LIBERO (nom.) | LP-Avg | Feature Dim |\n          |----------------------------|---------------|--------|-------------|\n          | CGS epistemic (full)       | 93.0          | 68.0   | 45D         |\n          | DiU aleatoric              | 94.5          | 65.0   | 7D          |\n          | Combined                   | 92.5          | 68.5   | 52D         |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig S3: Feature activation heatmap comparing epistemic vs aleatoric feature magnitudes on in-distribution vs OOD episodes. Epistemic features show clear elevation under shift; aleatoric features show mild elevation but less discrimination.\"\n    analysis: >\n      This ablation supports the architectural choice of CGS epistemic features over simpler aleatoric alternatives.\n      The 3pp gap is meaningful and distinguishes ACR-VLA from a DiU-based approach. The combined model's marginal\n      improvement (0.5pp) suggests the paper should not recommend the 52D variant as default — the extra 7 features\n      add complexity without commensurate gain. The key narrative point: \"CGS captures distribution shift through\n      inter-sample epistemic uncertainty, which is the primary challenge in LIBERO-Plus; aleatoric uncertainty\n      captures a complementary but less actionable signal under shift.\"\n\n  - name: \"CRM Feature Importance Analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_012\"\n    description: >\n      Gradient magnitude analysis of CRM's learned weights w.r.t. 45 uncertainty features, broken down by\n      perturbation type. Tests whether CRM uses conformal features in a dimension-selective manner.\n    expected_results:\n      top_5_features_by_gradient_magnitude:\n        - per_dof_std_joint1: 0.18\n        - per_dof_std_joint4: 0.15\n        - per_dof_std_joint6: 0.14\n        - ellipsoid_volume: 0.12\n        - per_dof_radius_joint1: 0.09\n      covariance_feature_total_importance_share_pct: 18.0\n      marginal_feature_total_importance_share_pct: 72.0\n      ellipsoid_volume_importance_share_pct: 10.0\n      dimension_selectivity_index: 0.35\n      perturbation_specific_feature_pattern_detected: false\n    result_rationale: >\n      In the median tier, CRM uses conformal features but not in a cleanly dimension-selective way. The top\n      features are per-DOF standard deviations (joints 1, 4, 6 — shoulder, elbow, wrist), which are marginal\n      statistics that don't require covariance structure. The ellipsoid volume (a summary statistic) ranks 4th,\n      acting as a global uncertainty indicator. Covariance-derived features (off-diagonal elements of the\n      shrunk covariance matrix, cross-DOF conformal radii) collectively account for only 18% of gradient\n      magnitude — present but not dominant. This is consistent with the median tier assumption that per-DOF\n      advantage comes primarily through marginal information. The dimension selectivity index (0.35 on a 0-1\n      scale where 1 = perfectly selective, 0 = uniform) indicates CRM pays more attention to certain DOFs\n      (proximal joints with higher variance under perturbation) but does not exhibit the clean \"correct only\n      the uncertain DOFs\" pattern that would support the stronger Claim 1 framing. No perturbation-specific\n      feature usage pattern is detected — CRM uses the same features similarly across perturbation types,\n      suggesting it learns a general correction strategy rather than perturbation-adaptive dimension selection.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 6: CRM Feature Importance (Top 10)\"\n        content: |\n          | Rank | Feature              | Grad. Mag. | Category    |\n          |------|----------------------|------------|-------------|\n          | 1    | std(joint1)          | 0.180      | Marginal    |\n          | 2    | std(joint4)          | 0.150      | Marginal    |\n          | 3    | std(joint6)          | 0.140      | Marginal    |\n          | 4    | ellipsoid_volume     | 0.120      | Summary     |\n          | 5    | radius(joint1)       | 0.090      | Marginal    |\n          | 6    | radius(joint4)       | 0.075      | Marginal    |\n          | 7    | std(joint5)          | 0.060      | Marginal    |\n          | 8    | cov(joint1,4)        | 0.045      | Covariance  |\n          | 9    | radius(joint6)       | 0.040      | Marginal    |\n          | 10   | cov(joint4,6)        | 0.035      | Covariance  |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 7a: Heatmap of gradient magnitudes (7 DOFs × 3 feature types + 1 summary) averaged across all perturbations. Shows concentration on per-DOF std with mild secondary attention to radii. Covariance row is faint.\"\n      - \"Fig 7b: Same heatmap split by perturbation type (7 sub-heatmaps). Patterns are similar across perturbation types — no clear perturbation-specific dimension selection. Some mild elevation of joint1 features under camera perturbation.\"\n    analysis: >\n      This is the analysis that forces the Claim 1 reframing. The feature importance pattern shows CRM uses\n      conformal features as general uncertainty indicators, not as dimension-selective correction signals.\n      Per-DOF std dominates because it's the most robust marginal statistic even with K=10 noise; covariance\n      features are used but weakly. The paper should present this honestly: \"CRM learns to condition corrections\n      on structured uncertainty features, with per-DOF standard deviations providing the primary signal.\n      Covariance-derived features contribute modestly (18% of gradient magnitude), suggesting that the full\n      multivariate CP formulation provides value primarily through richer marginal statistics rather than\n      cross-DOF correlation exploitation.\" This analysis is compatible with A4 showing only 2pp per-DOF vs\n      scalar gap — most of the value is in having per-DOF marginals, not in the covariance structure.\n\n  - name: \"SnapFlow Integration (K=10 × 1-step Distilled)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_013\"\n    description: >\n      Replace standard 10-step flow-matching sampling with SnapFlow 1-step distilled generation for K=10 samples.\n      Test whether sample diversity is preserved and measure inference speedup.\n    expected_results:\n      snapflow_liberoplus_avg_sr_pct: 67.0\n      snapflow_sr_drop_vs_standard_pp: 1.0\n      snapflow_libero_nominal_sr_pct: 92.5\n      snapflow_inference_time_ms: 18.0\n      standard_inference_time_ms: 58.0\n      snapflow_speedup_factor: 3.2\n      sample_diversity_kl_divergence_vs_standard: 0.08\n      cgs_ellipsoid_volume_snapflow_vs_standard_ratio: 0.92\n    result_rationale: >\n      SnapFlow 1-step distilled generation produces K=10 samples 3.2× faster than standard 10-step sampling,\n      reducing total CGS inference time from 58ms to 18ms (10 × 1-step vs 10 × 10-step). The 1pp SR drop\n      (67% vs 68%) comes from slightly reduced sample diversity: 1-step distilled generation produces samples\n      that are closer to the mode, reducing the tail variance that CGS needs to estimate covariance structure.\n      The CGS ellipsoid volume is 8% smaller with SnapFlow samples (0.92 ratio), meaning conformal sets are\n      slightly tighter — this is both because the true uncertainty is slightly underestimated (less diverse\n      samples) and because Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage has less variance to regularize. The KL divergence of 0.08\n      between SnapFlow and standard sample distributions indicates good but not perfect distribution matching.\n      For deployment, the 3.2× speedup with only 1pp SR trade-off is likely worthwhile, making ACR-VLA\n      feasible for real-time control at ~55Hz total pipeline throughput.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table S1: SnapFlow Integration\"\n        content: |\n          | Configuration           | LP-Avg | Inference (ms) | Speedup |\n          |-------------------------|--------|----------------|---------|\n          | Standard (10-step × 10) | 68.0   | 58.0           | 1.0×    |\n          | SnapFlow (1-step × 10)  | 67.0   | 18.0           | 3.2×    |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig S4: Scatter plot of sample diversity (x-axis: pairwise L2 distance between K=10 samples) vs CGS ellipsoid volume, colored by standard vs SnapFlow. SnapFlow cluster is shifted left (less diversity) and down (smaller ellipsoids) but largely overlapping.\"\n    analysis: >\n      SnapFlow integration is a practical contribution for deployment feasibility. The 1pp trade-off is acceptable\n      for most applications. In the paper, this should be positioned in the \"practical considerations\" section\n      as an optional efficiency optimization, not a core contribution. The result validates that CGS is compatible\n      with accelerated inference methods, broadening ACR-VLA's applicability.\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11185,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11185},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"eac149f7-0379-4dc7-b78d-7f9649494fc8","uuid":"a88d40a9-67fc-4a7d-bdb9-8a63467edea9"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":325252,"duration_api_ms":327360,"num_turns":1,"result":"```yaml\ntier: median\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  Claims need recalibration. Claim 1: reframe from \"per-DOF conformal features enable dimension-selective correction\"\n  to \"structured uncertainty features from multivariate CP provide complementary conditioning signal for learned\n  action correction.\" De-emphasize per-DOF vs scalar ablation (A4), emphasize conformal-vs-none ablation (A3).\n  Claim 2: downgrade COC from \"reliable self-referential signal\" to \"lightweight proxy enabling online adaptation\n  without external supervision\" — emphasize the no-supervision property rather than per-step reliability.\n  Claim 3: reframe as \"complementary in principle\" with caveat that training-time methods capture most low-hanging fruit.\n  Shift headline to CGS+CRM framework contribution. Feature importance analysis shows CRM uses conformal features\n  but not in a neatly dimension-selective way. Still a method paper, positioned more as architectural contribution\n  than empirical leap.\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: \"ACR-VLA Full Pipeline on SmolVLA\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_001\"\n    description: >\n      Full ACR-VLA pipeline: CGS (K=10, Ledoit-Wolf) + ACI/COC + CRM (131K params).\n      CRM trained on LIBERO demos + synthetic augmentation (camera jitter, Gaussian action noise, robot-state perturbation).\n      Evaluated on LIBERO 10-task nominal suite and LIBERO-Plus L3–L5.\n    expected_results:\n      base_smolvla_libero_nominal_sr_pct: 95.0\n      acrvla_libero_nominal_sr_pct: 93.0\n      libero_nominal_degradation_pp: 2.0\n      base_smolvla_liberoplus_avg_sr_pct: 62.0\n      base_smolvla_liberoplus_L3_sr_pct: 72.0\n      base_smolvla_liberoplus_L4_sr_pct: 60.0\n      base_smolvla_liberoplus_L5_sr_pct: 54.0\n      acrvla_liberoplus_avg_sr_pct: 68.0\n      acrvla_liberoplus_L3_sr_pct: 78.0\n      acrvla_liberoplus_L4_sr_pct: 66.0\n      acrvla_liberoplus_L5_sr_pct: 60.0\n      delta_over_base_liberoplus_sr_pp: 6.0\n      crm_inference_latency_ms: 1.8\n      cgs_inference_latency_ms: 12.5\n      total_pipeline_overhead_pct: 8.0\n    result_rationale: >\n      Median tier assumes CRM learns a useful correction but most gain comes from the observation-conditioned residual\n      pathway, not from conformal feature conditioning specifically. The 6pp gain over base reflects: (a) CRM successfully\n      learns to correct high-error actions using observation context (~3.5pp), (b) conformal features provide additional\n      complementary signal (~2.5pp via A3 gap), (c) ACI online adaptation contributes modest further improvement (~0.5-1pp).\n      K=10 covariance is noisy — Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage rescues the Mahalanobis ellipsoid from collapse but the\n      conformal radii carry less precise directional information than oracle covariance would. The 2pp nominal degradation\n      comes from CRM occasionally over-correcting on in-distribution steps where conformal features show mild uncertainty\n      (false positive corrections). Synthetic augmentation covers camera/noise/robot-state well but cannot cover layout\n      or language perturbations, limiting L5 gains. The pipeline clears the P0 threshold (>4pp gain) but falls short of\n      the optimistic tier's 8-12pp that would require conformal features to provide genuinely dimension-selective correction.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 1: Main Results — LIBERO and LIBERO-Plus Success Rate\"\n        content: |\n          | Method               | LIBERO (nom.) | LP-L3  | LP-L4  | LP-L5  | LP-Avg |\n          |----------------------|---------------|--------|--------|--------|--------|\n          | SmolVLA (base)       | 95.0          | 72.0   | 60.0   | 54.0   | 62.0   |\n          | + ACR-VLA (ours)     | 93.0          | 78.0   | 66.0   | 60.0   | 68.0   |\n          | Δ                    | -2.0          | +6.0   | +6.0   | +6.0   | +6.0   |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 2a: Bar chart comparing SmolVLA base vs ACR-VLA across LIBERO nominal and LIBERO-Plus L3/L4/L5. Shows consistent +6pp on all LP levels with -2pp nominal trade-off.\"\n      - \"Fig 2b: Per-task breakdown heatmap on LIBERO nominal (10 tasks × 2 methods). Shows ACR-VLA matches or slightly underperforms base on most tasks, with 1-3pp drops on 3-4 tasks where CRM over-corrects.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The 6pp average gain is real but moderate. Uniform improvement across L3-L5 suggests CRM learns a general\n      correction rather than dimension-specific adaptation — consistent with median tier's hypothesis that the gain\n      is primarily observation-conditioned. The 2pp nominal degradation is within acceptable range but noteworthy;\n      a gating mechanism or confidence threshold on CRM activation could reduce it. The result supports reframing\n      Claim 1 around \"structured uncertainty as complementary conditioning signal\" rather than \"dimension-selective correction.\"\n\n  - name: \"Ablation A3 — CRM without Conformal Features\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_002\"\n    description: >\n      CRM receives only observation features (visual embedding + proprioception + base action) but no CGS-derived\n      conformal statistics. Equivalent to an A2C2-style generic learned residual. MAKE-OR-BREAK for Claim 1.\n    expected_results:\n      a3_no_conformal_liberoplus_avg_sr_pct: 64.5\n      a3_gap_full_vs_no_conformal_pp: 3.5\n      a3_no_conformal_libero_nominal_sr_pct: 94.0\n      a3_no_conformal_liberoplus_L3_sr_pct: 74.0\n      a3_no_conformal_liberoplus_L4_sr_pct: 63.0\n      a3_no_conformal_liberoplus_L5_sr_pct: 56.5\n    result_rationale: >\n      The 3.5pp gap (64.5% vs 68.0%) confirms conformal features contribute meaningful signal beyond pure\n      observation-conditioned residual learning, but the gap is modest — in the [2,5] range rather than the >5pp\n      that would indicate strong dimension-selective correction. The A3 variant still achieves +2.5pp over bare\n      base (62%), showing that CRM's observation-conditioned residual learning captures substantial value independently.\n      This is the median tier's core insight: CRM is architecturally valuable as a learned correction module, and\n      conformal features improve it, but conformal conditioning is a complementary signal rather than the primary\n      driver. The gap passes the kill criterion (>2pp) so Claim 1 survives but requires reframing. Nominal\n      degradation is slightly lower (1pp vs 2pp) because without conformal features, CRM is less aggressive in\n      its corrections, producing fewer false-positive over-corrections on in-distribution data.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 2 (row): A3 ablation within main ablation table\"\n        content: |\n          | Variant                    | LIBERO (nom.) | LP-Avg | Δ vs Full |\n          |----------------------------|---------------|--------|-----------|\n          | ACR-VLA (full)             | 93.0          | 68.0   | —         |\n          | A3: CRM w/o conformal      | 94.0          | 64.5   | -3.5      |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 3 (ablation bar chart): A3 bar adjacent to full ACR-VLA and base. Clearly shows A3 intermediate between base and full, with 3.5pp gap. Error bars (95% CI over 3 seeds) likely overlap partially — gap is statistically significant but not dramatic.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The 3.5pp gap is the most important number in the paper. It clears the 2pp kill criterion but sits squarely\n      in the median range. In the paper, this should be presented as: \"removing conformal features reduces\n      LIBERO-Plus SR by 3.5pp, confirming that CGS-derived uncertainty statistics provide complementary conditioning\n      signal for CRM beyond what observation features alone can supply.\" Avoid framing as \"enabling dimension-selective\n      correction\" since the gap size and feature importance analysis (plan_012) won't support that stronger claim.\n      Reviewers may note that 3.5pp with overlapping CIs is modest — prepare to emphasize that this is a consistent\n      improvement across perturbation types and seeds, plus the architectural contribution argument.\n\n  - name: \"Ablation A4 — CRM with Scalar Uncertainty\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_003\"\n    description: >\n      CRM receives scalar uncertainty (Euclidean distance from calibration set mean, à la ReconVLA) instead of\n      45D per-DOF conformal features. Tests whether per-DOF structure provides value beyond a single scalar.\n    expected_results:\n      a4_scalar_liberoplus_avg_sr_pct: 66.0\n      a4_gap_perDOF_vs_scalar_pp: 2.0\n      a4_scalar_libero_nominal_sr_pct: 93.5\n      a4_scalar_liberoplus_L3_sr_pct: 76.0\n      a4_scalar_liberoplus_L4_sr_pct: 64.0\n      a4_scalar_liberoplus_L5_sr_pct: 58.0\n    result_rationale: >\n      The 2pp gap (66% vs 68%) confirms per-DOF features are better than scalar, but the margin is slim — consistent\n      with median tier's assumption that per-DOF advantage comes primarily through marginal information (per-DOF std\n      and radius independently) rather than cross-DOF covariance structure. A scalar summary statistic already\n      captures \"how uncertain is this action overall,\" which accounts for much of the useful signal. The remaining\n      2pp comes from CRM having access to which specific DOFs are uncertain, enabling modestly better residual\n      allocation. But because K=10 covariance is noisy, the rich covariance structure that would enable truly\n      dimension-selective correction is attenuated. The scalar variant (66%) vs no-conformal variant (64.5%) shows\n      only 1.5pp gap between scalar and no uncertainty — meaning even a crude uncertainty scalar helps, and the\n      per-DOF structure adds incrementally on top. This stacking of gaps (A3 → A4 → full) is consistent but\n      none of the individual gaps are large.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 2 (row): A4 ablation\"\n        content: |\n          | Variant                     | LIBERO (nom.) | LP-Avg | Δ vs Full |\n          |---------------------------- |---------------|--------|-----------|\n          | ACR-VLA (full, 45D)         | 93.0          | 68.0   | —         |\n          | A4: CRM w/ scalar uncert.   | 93.5          | 66.0   | -2.0      |\n          | A3: CRM w/o conformal       | 94.0          | 64.5   | -3.5      |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 3 (extended): Stacked bar or grouped bar showing base → A3 → A4 → full progression. Visualizes the incremental contribution: observation residual (+2.5), scalar uncertainty (+1.5), per-DOF structure (+2.0).\"\n    analysis: >\n      The A4 gap (2pp) is modest and should be de-emphasized in the paper per the narrative guidance. Rather than\n      claiming \"per-DOF > scalar,\" present it as part of the ablation ladder: \"uncertainty features improve CRM\n      regardless of representation (scalar +1.5pp, structured 45D +3.5pp over no uncertainty), with structured\n      features providing additional value through per-DOF granularity.\" The 2pp gap may not survive reviewer scrutiny\n      as a standalone claim — it likely requires 5+ seeds to achieve statistical significance. In the median tier,\n      this becomes supporting evidence rather than a headline result.\n\n  - name: \"ACR-VLA Stacked on SRPO\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_004\"\n    description: >\n      Train a new CRM on SRPO-finetuned SmolVLA checkpoint using LIBERO demos. Evaluate on LIBERO-Plus to test\n      orthogonal complementarity with training-time optimization (Claim 3).\n    expected_results:\n      srpo_base_liberoplus_avg_sr_pct: 82.1\n      srpo_acrvla_liberoplus_avg_sr_pct: 84.0\n      srpo_stacking_gain_pp: 1.9\n      srpo_acrvla_libero_nominal_sr_pct: 96.5\n      srpo_base_libero_nominal_sr_pct: 97.0\n      srpo_nominal_degradation_pp: 0.5\n      srpo_acrvla_liberoplus_L3_sr_pct: 89.0\n      srpo_acrvla_liberoplus_L4_sr_pct: 83.0\n      srpo_acrvla_liberoplus_L5_sr_pct: 80.0\n    result_rationale: >\n      SRPO's training-time optimization has already captured most of the correctable error — its 82.1% baseline\n      is 20pp above the base SmolVLA's 62%. The residual errors at 82.1% are predominantly from perturbation types\n      that synthetic augmentation cannot cover (layout changes, language variation) and from hard task instances\n      where the observation itself is insufficient. CRM's observation-conditioned residual finds less room to\n      improve because SRPO has already learned robust representations. The conformal features are also less\n      informative because SRPO's action distribution is already tighter under perturbation (lower epistemic\n      uncertainty → less signal for CGS). The 1.9pp stacking gain is real but marginal — consistent with median\n      tier's \"complementary in principle\" framing. The gain comes primarily from camera and noise perturbations\n      where synthetic augmentation provides coverage and CRM can still make useful corrections on top of SRPO.\n      Nominal degradation is only 0.5pp because SRPO's stronger base representations make CRM's false-positive\n      over-corrections less likely. The absolute 84.0% sets a new SOTA but the marginal gain is not compelling\n      as a standalone selling point.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 3: Stacking on SRPO\"\n        content: |\n          | Method                  | LIBERO (nom.) | LP-Avg | LP-L3 | LP-L4 | LP-L5 |\n          |-------------------------|---------------|--------|-------|-------|-------|\n          | SRPO (SOTA)             | 97.0          | 82.1   | 87.0  | 81.0  | 78.3  |\n          | SRPO + ACR-VLA (ours)   | 96.5          | 84.0   | 89.0  | 83.0  | 80.0  |\n          | Δ                       | -0.5          | +1.9   | +2.0  | +2.0  | +1.7  |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 4: Comparison chart showing ACR-VLA on SmolVLA base (+6pp) vs ACR-VLA on SRPO (+1.9pp). Illustrates diminishing returns as base improves, supporting 'complementary in principle' framing.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The 1.9pp stacking gain falls below the 2pp threshold in the kill criterion for Claim 3, putting the claim\n      in a borderline position. With standard error bars over 3 seeds, 1.9pp may or may not be statistically\n      significant. In the median tier, Claim 3 is downgraded to \"complementary in principle\" — the paper should\n      acknowledge that SRPO captures most correctable error and position stacking as demonstrating architectural\n      compatibility rather than a compelling empirical lift. The headline result shifts to the +6pp on base SmolVLA\n      as the primary contribution, with SRPO stacking as a secondary data point. The new SOTA of 84.0% is still\n      reportable but should not be the paper's primary selling point.\n\n  - name: \"COC Signal Validation\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_005\"\n    description: >\n      Validate Chunk Overlap Consistency as self-referential ACI signal: (a) Spearman ρ(COC coverage fraction,\n      task success) across episodes, (b) ACI α_t convergence trajectory, (c) ACI-adapted ellipsoid volume vs\n      static calibration at matched empirical coverage.\n    expected_results:\n      coc_success_spearman_rho: 0.40\n      coc_success_spearman_p_value: 0.002\n      aci_converges_within_episodes: 120\n      aci_steady_state_alpha: 0.12\n      aci_target_coverage: 0.90\n      aci_achieved_coverage_mean: 0.88\n      aci_achieved_coverage_std: 0.04\n      ellipsoid_volume_static_calibration: 1.0\n      ellipsoid_volume_aci_adapted_relative: 0.85\n      aci_ellipsoid_volume_narrowing_pct: 15.0\n      coc_per_step_accuracy_as_binary_classifier: 0.62\n    result_rationale: >\n      COC correlates with task success at ρ=0.40 — statistically significant but weak. Episodes with consistently\n      high COC agreement (overlapping action chunks agree with each other) tend to succeed more often, but the\n      per-step signal is noisy (62% accuracy as binary success predictor). This is because chunk overlap measures\n      temporal consistency of the policy, which correlates with but does not cause success — a policy can be\n      consistently wrong (high COC, low success) or succeed despite occasional inconsistency. ACI converges\n      within ~120 episodes because the weak but consistent signal is sufficient for slow exponential-moving-average\n      adaptation of the conformal threshold α_t. The 15% ellipsoid volume narrowing shows ACI tightens prediction\n      sets modestly compared to static calibration — it detects distribution shift and adjusts, but the noisy\n      COC signal prevents aggressive tightening. Achieved coverage (88%) undershoots the 90% target slightly\n      due to COC noise causing occasional over-tightening. This profile supports reframing COC as \"lightweight\n      proxy enabling online adaptation without external supervision\" — the key value is not per-step reliability\n      but the ability to adapt without any ground-truth task success signal.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 4: COC Signal Quality\"\n        content: |\n          | Metric                              | Value         |\n          |-------------------------------------|---------------|\n          | Spearman ρ (COC, task success)       | 0.40 (p=.002) |\n          | COC per-step binary accuracy         | 62%           |\n          | ACI convergence episodes             | ~120          |\n          | ACI target / achieved coverage       | 90% / 88±4%  |\n          | Ellipsoid volume narrowing (vs static)| 15%          |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 5a: Scatter plot of episode-level COC coverage fraction vs binary task success. Shows positive trend (ρ=0.40) with substantial scatter — many episodes with high COC still fail, and some low-COC episodes succeed.\"\n      - \"Fig 5b: ACI α_t convergence trajectory over 200 episodes. Shows initial oscillation (ep 0-50), gradual convergence (ep 50-120), and steady-state fluctuation around α=0.12 (ep 120+).\"\n      - \"Fig 5c: Violin plot comparing ellipsoid volumes under static calibration vs ACI-adapted, at matched ~88% empirical coverage. ACI distribution is tighter (15% narrower) but with longer tail.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The ρ=0.40 result is the best the paper can honestly report for COC as a self-referential signal. It clears\n      the 0.3 kill threshold but is far from the 0.6+ that would support \"reliable\" characterization. The paper\n      should position this as: \"COC provides a lightweight, supervision-free proxy for ACI adaptation that achieves\n      meaningful ellipsoid tightening (15%) without requiring any external task success signal — trading per-step\n      reliability for complete autonomy.\" The 62% per-step accuracy should be reported honestly as a limitation.\n      The convergence within 120 episodes is adequate for deployment scenarios but means ACI is a slow-adaptation\n      mechanism, not a real-time reliability signal.\n\n  - name: \"Ablation A1 — CRM without ACI (Static Calibration)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_006\"\n    description: >\n      CRM with static CGS calibration (fixed α from calibration set, no ACI online adaptation).\n      Isolates the contribution of ACI/COC online threshold adjustment.\n    expected_results:\n      a1_static_liberoplus_avg_sr_pct: 66.5\n      a1_static_libero_nominal_sr_pct: 93.0\n      a1_gap_vs_full_pp: 1.5\n      a1_static_liberoplus_L3_sr_pct: 77.0\n      a1_static_liberoplus_L4_sr_pct: 65.0\n      a1_static_liberoplus_L5_sr_pct: 57.5\n    result_rationale: >\n      ACI's online adaptation contributes a modest 1.5pp over static calibration. Static CGS with a fixed threshold\n      calibrated on the nominal distribution still produces useful conformal features — the per-DOF std and radius\n      estimates are driven primarily by sample diversity rather than threshold tuning. Under distribution shift,\n      static calibration's conformal sets are overly conservative (too wide ellipsoids), meaning CRM receives\n      inflated uncertainty features that reduce its correction precision. ACI's 15% volume narrowing translates\n      to CRM making slightly more targeted corrections. However, the improvement is modest because (a) CRM can\n      partially compensate for miscalibrated features by learning to discount inflated values during training,\n      and (b) the ACI adaptation is itself noisy (COC ρ=0.40), so it only partially corrects the calibration\n      error. The 1.5pp gap is consistent across perturbation levels, suggesting ACI provides uniform rather than\n      perturbation-specific improvement.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 2 (row): A1 ablation\"\n        content: |\n          | Variant                    | LIBERO (nom.) | LP-Avg | Δ vs Full |\n          |----------------------------|---------------|--------|-----------|\n          | ACR-VLA (full)             | 93.0          | 68.0   | —         |\n          | A1: Static calibration     | 93.0          | 66.5   | -1.5      |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 3 (ablation chart, A1 bar): Shows A1 slightly below full system. The gap is small but consistent across seeds.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The 1.5pp gap is small enough that ACI is a \"nice to have\" rather than a critical component. This is consistent\n      with median tier: COC is noisy, so ACI's adaptation is limited. In the paper, present ACI as providing\n      \"principled online recalibration\" with the honest caveat that the empirical gain is modest. The value\n      proposition is more about the theoretical coverage guarantee than the empirical SR improvement.\n\n  - name: \"Ablation A2 — ACI without CRM (Sample Selection Only)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_007\"\n    description: >\n      CGS + COC/ACI used for sample selection (pick lowest-uncertainty sample from K=10 candidates) without\n      CRM residual correction. Extends ReconVLA's approach to multivariate conformal setting.\n    expected_results:\n      a2_selection_liberoplus_avg_sr_pct: 64.0\n      a2_selection_libero_nominal_sr_pct: 95.5\n      a2_gap_vs_full_pp: 4.0\n      a2_gap_vs_base_pp: 2.0\n      a2_selection_liberoplus_L3_sr_pct: 73.5\n      a2_selection_liberoplus_L4_sr_pct: 62.0\n      a2_selection_liberoplus_L5_sr_pct: 56.5\n    result_rationale: >\n      Sample selection from K=10 flow-matching candidates provides modest 2pp improvement over base — CGS\n      identifies which samples have lower joint nonconformity and selecting them avoids the worst outlier\n      actions. But this approach is fundamentally limited: it can only select among existing samples, not\n      synthesize a correction. When all 10 samples are biased in the same direction (common under systematic\n      perturbation), selection cannot help. CRM's learned residual adds the crucial ability to push the action\n      toward a corrected target, accounting for the remaining 4pp gap to full ACR-VLA. The near-zero nominal\n      degradation (-0.5pp from 95.5% vs 95.0%) is because sample selection is conservative — it rarely makes\n      things worse since it's choosing among valid samples. This ablation is the strongest evidence for CRM's\n      value: the learned correction module provides 4pp of the total 6pp gain, while sample selection provides only 2pp.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 2 (row): A2 ablation\"\n        content: |\n          | Variant                        | LIBERO (nom.) | LP-Avg | Δ vs Full | Δ vs Base |\n          |-------------------------------|---------------|--------|-----------|-----------|\n          | ACR-VLA (full)                | 93.0          | 68.0   | —         | +6.0      |\n          | A2: Sample selection only      | 95.5          | 64.0   | -4.0      | +2.0      |\n          | SmolVLA (base)                | 95.0          | 62.0   | -6.0      | —         |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 3 (ablation chart, A2 bar): A2 is close to base, far from full system. Visually makes the case for CRM's importance.\"\n    analysis: >\n      This ablation is critical for the paper's architecture narrative. It shows that the CRM learned correction\n      module — not sample selection — is the primary source of improvement. Combined with A3 (CRM w/o conformal\n      features at 64.5%), it reveals an interesting decomposition: sample selection (+2pp) ≈ CRM w/o conformal (+2.5pp),\n      meaning CGS-informed sample selection and observation-conditioned residual provide similar value. CRM with\n      conformal features (+6pp total) outperforms both, confirming the value of combining learned correction with\n      structured uncertainty conditioning.\n\n  - name: \"Per-Perturbation Breakdown\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_008\"\n    description: >\n      ACR-VLA gain broken down across 7 LIBERO-Plus perturbation dimensions: camera jitter, observation noise,\n      robot-state perturbation, lighting, background, layout, language. Tests hypothesis that gains concentrate\n      on augmentation-covered perturbations.\n    expected_results:\n      gain_camera_jitter_pp: 9.0\n      gain_observation_noise_pp: 10.0\n      gain_robot_state_pp: 7.0\n      gain_lighting_pp: 5.0\n      gain_background_pp: 4.0\n      gain_layout_pp: 2.0\n      gain_language_pp: 1.0\n      avg_augmented_perturbations_gain_pp: 8.7\n      avg_non_augmented_perturbations_gain_pp: 2.3\n    result_rationale: >\n      Gains strongly correlate with synthetic augmentation coverage. Camera jitter (+9pp) and observation noise\n      (+10pp) are directly covered by training-time augmentation, so CRM has seen similar perturbations during\n      training and learns effective corrections. Robot-state perturbation (+7pp) is partially covered (joint-level\n      noise augmentation captures some but not all dynamics changes). Lighting (+5pp) and background (+4pp) are\n      visual perturbations where CRM benefits from visual encoder features detecting anomalous observations,\n      even without explicit augmentation. Layout (+2pp) and language (+1pp) show minimal improvement because\n      these perturbation types change task semantics in ways that neither synthetic augmentation nor conformal\n      statistics can address — a rearranged scene or rephrased instruction requires different behavior, not\n      corrected behavior. The camera/noise/robot-state average of 8.7pp is strong and within the [5,12] range\n      specified for median tier. The layout/language near-zero gain is an honest limitation that should be\n      reported transparently.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 5: Per-Perturbation ACR-VLA Gain\"\n        content: |\n          | Perturbation     | Base SR | ACR-VLA SR | Δ (pp)  | Augmented? |\n          |------------------|---------|------------|---------|------------|\n          | Camera jitter    | 58.0    | 67.0       | +9.0    | ✓          |\n          | Obs. noise       | 55.0    | 65.0       | +10.0   | ✓          |\n          | Robot-state      | 60.0    | 67.0       | +7.0    | ✓ (partial)|\n          | Lighting         | 65.0    | 70.0       | +5.0    | ✗          |\n          | Background       | 68.0    | 72.0       | +4.0    | ✗          |\n          | Layout           | 62.0    | 64.0       | +2.0    | ✗          |\n          | Language         | 66.0    | 67.0       | +1.0    | ✗          |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 6: Grouped bar chart (7 perturbation types × base/ACR-VLA). Color-coded by augmentation coverage (green=covered, yellow=partial, red=uncovered). Clear visual pattern: gains decrease from left to right as augmentation coverage decreases.\"\n      - \"Fig 6 inset: Scatter plot of augmentation coverage (binary/partial) vs SR gain. Shows strong correlation, supporting the honest-limitations narrative.\"\n    analysis: >\n      This breakdown is both a strength and vulnerability. Strength: camera/noise/robot-state gains are substantial\n      (7-10pp) and clearly demonstrate ACR-VLA's value for the perturbation types it's designed to handle. Vulnerability:\n      layout/language near-zero gains reveal that ACR-VLA (and CGS more broadly) cannot address semantic perturbations.\n      Reviewers will likely probe this limitation. The paper should position it honestly: \"ACR-VLA is most effective\n      for perturbations that induce distributional shift in the action space without changing task semantics — camera\n      distortion, sensor noise, and kinematic perturbation. For perturbations that alter scene layout or language\n      instructions, training-time methods (SRPO, RobustVLA) that modify the policy's learned representations are\n      more appropriate.\" The per-perturbation SRPO stacking breakdown should also be reported (plan_004 subset)\n      to show whether stacking helps specifically on the augmentation-covered perturbations.\n\n  - name: \"Ablation A5 — Joint Mahalanobis vs Bonferroni Correction\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_009\"\n    description: >\n      Compare CGS with joint Mahalanobis nonconformity score vs per-DOF Bonferroni correction\n      (independent conformal intervals per dimension with α/7 correction). Tests whether the\n      multivariate CP formulation adds value over independent per-DOF intervals.\n    expected_results:\n      a5_bonferroni_liberoplus_avg_sr_pct: 66.5\n      a5_joint_mahalanobis_liberoplus_avg_sr_pct: 68.0\n      a5_gap_joint_vs_bonferroni_pp: 1.5\n      bonferroni_ellipsoid_volume_ratio_vs_joint: 1.35\n      bonferroni_coverage_achieved_pct: 93.0\n      joint_coverage_achieved_pct: 90.0\n    result_rationale: >\n      Joint Mahalanobis provides 1.5pp advantage over Bonferroni. In the median tier, K=10 covariance is noisy,\n      so the Mahalanobis ellipsoid is not dramatically tighter than the Bonferroni hypercube. With oracle\n      covariance, the joint approach would exploit cross-DOF correlations to produce much tighter ellipsoids;\n      with K=10 and Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage, the covariance estimate is regularized toward the identity, partially\n      undoing the advantage. Bonferroni's α/7 correction makes each marginal interval conservative, leading to\n      35% larger total prediction set volume and 93% coverage (above 90% target — over-coverage wastes\n      information). The joint approach's tighter ellipsoid gives CRM more precise conformal features, particularly\n      for DOFs with strong correlations (e.g., shoulder-elbow joint coupling). But the advantage is modest because\n      Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage attenuates exactly the cross-DOF correlation structure that makes joint Mahalanobis\n      superior to Bonferroni.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 2 (row): A5 ablation\"\n        content: |\n          | Variant                         | LP-Avg | Coverage | Volume Ratio |\n          |---------------------------------|--------|----------|-------------|\n          | CGS (joint Mahalanobis)         | 68.0   | 90%      | 1.00        |\n          | A5: Bonferroni per-DOF          | 66.5   | 93%      | 1.35        |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig S1 (supplementary): Ellipsoid vs Bonferroni hypercube visualization for representative 2-DOF slice (DOF 1 vs DOF 4). Shows tighter joint ellipsoid capturing correlation structure vs axis-aligned rectangle.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The 1.5pp gap is modest and supports the median tier narrative. The advantage exists but is attenuated by\n      K=10 covariance estimation noise. This is a P2 ablation that belongs in supplementary material. It validates\n      the design choice for joint Mahalanobis but does not constitute a major finding. If reviewers push on K=10\n      covariance reliability, this ablation shows the approach still beats the simpler alternative despite\n      estimation noise, which is a reasonable defense.\n\n  - name: \"Ablation A6 — COC vs Episode-Level Success for ACI\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_010\"\n    description: >\n      Compare ACI updated with COC (self-referential, no supervision) vs ACI updated with\n      episode-level binary task success (oracle signal). Tests whether COC's noise penalty\n      is offset by its supervision-free property.\n    expected_results:\n      a6_coc_aci_liberoplus_avg_sr_pct: 68.0\n      a6_oracle_aci_liberoplus_avg_sr_pct: 69.5\n      a6_gap_oracle_vs_coc_pp: 1.5\n      a6_coc_aci_convergence_episodes: 120\n      a6_oracle_aci_convergence_episodes: 40\n      a6_coc_coverage_std: 0.04\n      a6_oracle_coverage_std: 0.02\n    result_rationale: >\n      Oracle episode-level success provides a cleaner ACI signal, achieving 1.5pp higher SR and converging 3×\n      faster (40 vs 120 episodes). This is expected: binary success is a direct, noise-free indicator of whether\n      the policy is performing well, while COC is a proxy that correlates weakly (ρ=0.40). The coverage standard\n      deviation is also halved with oracle signal (0.02 vs 0.04), meaning ACI is more stable. However, the\n      performance gap is only 1.5pp, which demonstrates that COC's weak but consistent correlation with success\n      is sufficient for ACI to eventually converge to a reasonable threshold. The key argument for COC is not\n      performance parity but the supervision-free property: episode-level success requires an external reward\n      signal that is unavailable during deployment. COC operates on the policy's own output consistency, making\n      it applicable without any environment reward. The 1.5pp price of supervision-freedom is a reasonable\n      trade-off for deployment autonomy.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 4 (extended): ACI Signal Comparison\"\n        content: |\n          | ACI Signal         | LP-Avg | Convergence | Coverage σ | Supervision |\n          |--------------------|--------|-------------|------------|-------------|\n          | COC (ours)         | 68.0   | ~120 ep     | 0.04       | None        |\n          | Episode success    | 69.5   | ~40 ep      | 0.02       | Required    |\n          | Δ                  | -1.5   | —           | —          | —           |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig S2a: ACI α_t convergence curves for COC vs oracle signal. Oracle converges faster and with less oscillation.\"\n      - \"Fig S2b: Running coverage (50-episode window) for both signals. Oracle maintains tighter band around 90% target.\"\n    analysis: >\n      This ablation validates the design choice of COC for ACI. The 1.5pp gap is the \"price of autonomy\" — the\n      paper should frame it as: \"COC trades 1.5pp in SR for complete independence from external supervision,\n      enabling ACI adaptation during deployment without environment reward access.\" This is a stronger argument\n      than claiming COC is a \"reliable\" signal — it's a sufficient signal for a task (slow threshold adaptation)\n      that is tolerant of noise.\n\n  - name: \"Ablation A7 — DiU Aleatoric vs CGS Epistemic Features\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_011\"\n    description: >\n      Compare CRM conditioned on: (a) CGS epistemic features (45D, full system), (b) DiU single-pass aleatoric\n      features (7D variance estimate), (c) combined epistemic+aleatoric (52D). Tests whether epistemic uncertainty\n      from conformal prediction provides distinct value over aleatoric uncertainty estimation.\n    expected_results:\n      a7_cgs_epistemic_45d_liberoplus_avg_sr_pct: 68.0\n      a7_diu_aleatoric_7d_liberoplus_avg_sr_pct: 65.0\n      a7_combined_52d_liberoplus_avg_sr_pct: 68.5\n      a7_gap_epistemic_vs_aleatoric_pp: 3.0\n      a7_combined_vs_epistemic_pp: 0.5\n      a7_diu_libero_nominal_sr_pct: 94.5\n      a7_combined_libero_nominal_sr_pct: 92.5\n    result_rationale: >\n      CGS epistemic features outperform DiU aleatoric features by 3pp on LIBERO-Plus. Under distribution shift,\n      epistemic uncertainty rises (the model encounters unfamiliar observations, producing diverse action samples\n      with high inter-sample variance), providing a strong signal for CRM to detect and correct. DiU's aleatoric\n      estimate captures intrinsic action variance that is present even in-distribution — this is useful for\n      identifying inherently ambiguous actions but does not specifically detect distribution shift. On LIBERO-Plus,\n      where the challenge is distribution shift rather than intrinsic ambiguity, epistemic features are more\n      informative. The combined 52D model shows only 0.5pp improvement over epistemic-only, suggesting the\n      aleatoric signal is largely redundant when epistemic features are available — epistemic uncertainty subsumes\n      the actionable information in aleatoric estimates under shift. The combined model also shows slightly more\n      nominal degradation (92.5% vs 93.0%) due to the additional aleatoric features sometimes triggering\n      unnecessary corrections on in-distribution data where aleatoric uncertainty is naturally elevated.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 2 (row): A7 ablation\"\n        content: |\n          | Variant                    | LIBERO (nom.) | LP-Avg | Feature Dim |\n          |----------------------------|---------------|--------|-------------|\n          | CGS epistemic (full)       | 93.0          | 68.0   | 45D         |\n          | DiU aleatoric              | 94.5          | 65.0   | 7D          |\n          | Combined                   | 92.5          | 68.5   | 52D         |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig S3: Feature activation heatmap comparing epistemic vs aleatoric feature magnitudes on in-distribution vs OOD episodes. Epistemic features show clear elevation under shift; aleatoric features show mild elevation but less discrimination.\"\n    analysis: >\n      This ablation supports the architectural choice of CGS epistemic features over simpler aleatoric alternatives.\n      The 3pp gap is meaningful and distinguishes ACR-VLA from a DiU-based approach. The combined model's marginal\n      improvement (0.5pp) suggests the paper should not recommend the 52D variant as default — the extra 7 features\n      add complexity without commensurate gain. The key narrative point: \"CGS captures distribution shift through\n      inter-sample epistemic uncertainty, which is the primary challenge in LIBERO-Plus; aleatoric uncertainty\n      captures a complementary but less actionable signal under shift.\"\n\n  - name: \"CRM Feature Importance Analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_012\"\n    description: >\n      Gradient magnitude analysis of CRM's learned weights w.r.t. 45 uncertainty features, broken down by\n      perturbation type. Tests whether CRM uses conformal features in a dimension-selective manner.\n    expected_results:\n      top_5_features_by_gradient_magnitude:\n        - per_dof_std_joint1: 0.18\n        - per_dof_std_joint4: 0.15\n        - per_dof_std_joint6: 0.14\n        - ellipsoid_volume: 0.12\n        - per_dof_radius_joint1: 0.09\n      covariance_feature_total_importance_share_pct: 18.0\n      marginal_feature_total_importance_share_pct: 72.0\n      ellipsoid_volume_importance_share_pct: 10.0\n      dimension_selectivity_index: 0.35\n      perturbation_specific_feature_pattern_detected: false\n    result_rationale: >\n      In the median tier, CRM uses conformal features but not in a cleanly dimension-selective way. The top\n      features are per-DOF standard deviations (joints 1, 4, 6 — shoulder, elbow, wrist), which are marginal\n      statistics that don't require covariance structure. The ellipsoid volume (a summary statistic) ranks 4th,\n      acting as a global uncertainty indicator. Covariance-derived features (off-diagonal elements of the\n      shrunk covariance matrix, cross-DOF conformal radii) collectively account for only 18% of gradient\n      magnitude — present but not dominant. This is consistent with the median tier assumption that per-DOF\n      advantage comes primarily through marginal information. The dimension selectivity index (0.35 on a 0-1\n      scale where 1 = perfectly selective, 0 = uniform) indicates CRM pays more attention to certain DOFs\n      (proximal joints with higher variance under perturbation) but does not exhibit the clean \"correct only\n      the uncertain DOFs\" pattern that would support the stronger Claim 1 framing. No perturbation-specific\n      feature usage pattern is detected — CRM uses the same features similarly across perturbation types,\n      suggesting it learns a general correction strategy rather than perturbation-adaptive dimension selection.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 6: CRM Feature Importance (Top 10)\"\n        content: |\n          | Rank | Feature              | Grad. Mag. | Category    |\n          |------|----------------------|------------|-------------|\n          | 1    | std(joint1)          | 0.180      | Marginal    |\n          | 2    | std(joint4)          | 0.150      | Marginal    |\n          | 3    | std(joint6)          | 0.140      | Marginal    |\n          | 4    | ellipsoid_volume     | 0.120      | Summary     |\n          | 5    | radius(joint1)       | 0.090      | Marginal    |\n          | 6    | radius(joint4)       | 0.075      | Marginal    |\n          | 7    | std(joint5)          | 0.060      | Marginal    |\n          | 8    | cov(joint1,4)        | 0.045      | Covariance  |\n          | 9    | radius(joint6)       | 0.040      | Marginal    |\n          | 10   | cov(joint4,6)        | 0.035      | Covariance  |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 7a: Heatmap of gradient magnitudes (7 DOFs × 3 feature types + 1 summary) averaged across all perturbations. Shows concentration on per-DOF std with mild secondary attention to radii. Covariance row is faint.\"\n      - \"Fig 7b: Same heatmap split by perturbation type (7 sub-heatmaps). Patterns are similar across perturbation types — no clear perturbation-specific dimension selection. Some mild elevation of joint1 features under camera perturbation.\"\n    analysis: >\n      This is the analysis that forces the Claim 1 reframing. The feature importance pattern shows CRM uses\n      conformal features as general uncertainty indicators, not as dimension-selective correction signals.\n      Per-DOF std dominates because it's the most robust marginal statistic even with K=10 noise; covariance\n      features are used but weakly. The paper should present this honestly: \"CRM learns to condition corrections\n      on structured uncertainty features, with per-DOF standard deviations providing the primary signal.\n      Covariance-derived features contribute modestly (18% of gradient magnitude), suggesting that the full\n      multivariate CP formulation provides value primarily through richer marginal statistics rather than\n      cross-DOF correlation exploitation.\" This analysis is compatible with A4 showing only 2pp per-DOF vs\n      scalar gap — most of the value is in having per-DOF marginals, not in the covariance structure.\n\n  - name: \"SnapFlow Integration (K=10 × 1-step Distilled)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_013\"\n    description: >\n      Replace standard 10-step flow-matching sampling with SnapFlow 1-step distilled generation for K=10 samples.\n      Test whether sample diversity is preserved and measure inference speedup.\n    expected_results:\n      snapflow_liberoplus_avg_sr_pct: 67.0\n      snapflow_sr_drop_vs_standard_pp: 1.0\n      snapflow_libero_nominal_sr_pct: 92.5\n      snapflow_inference_time_ms: 18.0\n      standard_inference_time_ms: 58.0\n      snapflow_speedup_factor: 3.2\n      sample_diversity_kl_divergence_vs_standard: 0.08\n      cgs_ellipsoid_volume_snapflow_vs_standard_ratio: 0.92\n    result_rationale: >\n      SnapFlow 1-step distilled generation produces K=10 samples 3.2× faster than standard 10-step sampling,\n      reducing total CGS inference time from 58ms to 18ms (10 × 1-step vs 10 × 10-step). The 1pp SR drop\n      (67% vs 68%) comes from slightly reduced sample diversity: 1-step distilled generation produces samples\n      that are closer to the mode, reducing the tail variance that CGS needs to estimate covariance structure.\n      The CGS ellipsoid volume is 8% smaller with SnapFlow samples (0.92 ratio), meaning conformal sets are\n      slightly tighter — this is both because the true uncertainty is slightly underestimated (less diverse\n      samples) and because Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage has less variance to regularize. The KL divergence of 0.08\n      between SnapFlow and standard sample distributions indicates good but not perfect distribution matching.\n      For deployment, the 3.2× speedup with only 1pp SR trade-off is likely worthwhile, making ACR-VLA\n      feasible for real-time control at ~55Hz total pipeline throughput.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table S1: SnapFlow Integration\"\n        content: |\n          | Configuration           | LP-Avg | Inference (ms) | Speedup |\n          |-------------------------|--------|----------------|---------|\n          | Standard (10-step × 10) | 68.0   | 58.0           | 1.0×    |\n          | SnapFlow (1-step × 10)  | 67.0   | 18.0           | 3.2×    |\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig S4: Scatter plot of sample diversity (x-axis: pairwise L2 distance between K=10 samples) vs CGS ellipsoid volume, colored by standard vs SnapFlow. SnapFlow cluster is shifted left (less diversity) and down (smaller ellipsoids) but largely overlapping.\"\n    analysis: >\n      SnapFlow integration is a practical contribution for deployment feasibility. The 1pp trade-off is acceptable\n      for most applications. In the paper, this should be positioned in the \"practical considerations\" section\n      as an optional efficiency optimization, not a core contribution. The result validates that CGS is compatible\n      with accelerated inference methods, broadening ACR-VLA's applicability.\n```","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"eac149f7-0379-4dc7-b78d-7f9649494fc8","total_cost_usd":0.4842997500000001,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11185,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"output_tokens":16088,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11185,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":16088,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11185,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11185},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":6143,"outputTokens":24,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.006263,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000},"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":16088,"cacheReadInputTokens":11841,"cacheCreationInputTokens":11185,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.47803675000000007,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"669d9841-4018-4fce-ac0c-16655c965e90"}
